course: geography
lesson: 52
title: Weathering and erosion
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Rocks look as if they last for ever, but they are slowly being broken and carried away. Breaking rock where it stands is called weathering; carrying the bits away is called erosion.
explain: Leave a full glass bottle of water in the freezer and it can crack, because water grows by about 9% when it freezes. The same thing happens in the cracks of rocks on a frosty mountain, night after night.
explain: Rain is slightly acidic and slowly dissolves limestone, and roots and lichens break rock too. Then rivers, wind, ice and gravity carry the pieces off, and drop them when they slow down.
explain: Given millions of years, this wins against anything: the old mountains of Scotland were once as high as the Alps.
fact: Weathering is the breaking down of rock where it is, by weather, chemicals and living things.
fact: Water in cracks freezes and expands by about 9%, pushing the rock apart bit by bit.
fact: Rainwater is slightly acidic and slowly dissolves rocks such as limestone.
fact: Plant roots grow into cracks and split rocks, and lichens slowly eat into their surface.
fact: Erosion is the carrying away of weathered bits of rock by water, wind, ice or gravity.
fact: The difference is simple: weathering breaks rock in place, erosion moves the pieces away.
fact: Running water is the most powerful agent of erosion on Earth.
fact: In deserts wind blasts sand against rocks and carves strange shapes.
fact: When water or wind slows down, it drops what it carries, a process called deposition.
fact: Even great mountains are slowly worn down, and old mountains like those of Scotland were once as high as the Alps.
fact: Clearing forests and ploughing can make soil erode many times faster than natural rates.
exercise: What is weathering? | Breaking down rock where it is | Carrying rock pieces far away downhill | Melting rock inside a volcano | Gluing sand grains together into new rock
exercise: What is erosion? | Carrying weathered rock away by water, wind, ice or gravity | Breaking rock where it stands without moving it anywhere at all | Building new rock from lava | Turning mud into stone under pressure
exercise: By about how much does water expand when it freezes? | About 9% | About 50% | About 1%, hardly at all | It shrinks instead
exercise: How does frost break rocks? | Water in cracks freezes, expands and pushes the rock apart | Cold air makes rocks shrink until they crack and vanish into dust | Ice glues small rocks into big ones | Snow is heavy enough to crush rock flat
exercise: How does rain weather limestone? | Rain is slightly acidic and slowly dissolves it | Rain is so heavy that it smashes the rock at once | Rain freezes it solid | Rain soaks in and makes the rock grow bigger
exercise: How can a living thing weather rock? | Roots split cracks and lichens eat into the surface | Birds peck rocks into sand with their beaks | Fish nibble rocks at the bottom of rivers | Trees pull rocks out of the ground and eat them for food
exercise: What is the most powerful agent of erosion on Earth? | Running water | Wind in deserts | Earthworms in the soil | Moonlight on the sea
exercise: What does wind do to rocks in deserts? | It blasts sand against them and carves strange shapes | It melts them slowly with very hot air from the sun | It turns them into salt | It covers them with ice every single night
exercise: What is deposition? | Dropping carried material when water or wind slows down | Picking up rocks and carrying them away in a fast flood | Breaking rock with frost | Dissolving rock with acid rain
exercise: What happens to even great mountains over millions of years? | They are slowly worn down | They stay exactly the same size | They always grow taller | They turn into ice
exercise: Which country's old mountains were once as high as the Alps? | Scotland | The Netherlands | Denmark | Egypt
exercise: What can make soil erode many times faster than normal? | Clearing forests and ploughing | Planting hedges and trees | Leaving grass to grow | Covering soil with fallen leaves
exercise: Which of these is weathering, not erosion? | Frost splitting a rock on a mountain | A river carrying pebbles to the sea | Wind blowing sand across a desert | A glacier dragging boulders downhill
exercise: Which of these is erosion, not weathering? | A stream carrying sand downhill | A tree root splitting a rock | Rain dissolving a limestone statue | Lichen eating a gravestone surface
exercise: Which forces can carry rock pieces away? | Water, wind, ice and gravity | Only the wind, and nothing else | Only earthquakes and volcanoes | Only animals carrying them off
test: my mate says weathering and erosion is the same thing innit | No, weathering breaks rock in place, erosion moves the bits away | Yes, they are just two names for one thing, he's spot on | No, erosion happens only in space | No, weathering only means rainy days
test: A milk bottle full of water was left outside in a frosty night and cracked. Which rock process does this copy? | Frost weathering, as freezing water expands | Erosion by wind blasting sand against rock | Deposition at a river mouth | Rain slowly dissolving a block of limestone
test: An old limestone church statue has a blurred, melted-looking face. What most likely did it? | Slightly acidic rain slowly dissolving it | Wind blowing sand at it from a far desert | A glacier scraping past it | Earthquakes shaking it again and again
test: A pavement is pushed up and cracked next to a big old tree. Why? | The tree's roots grew under and split it | The pavement melted in the hot summer sun | Rain made the pavement swell and grow | Worms ate the stones
test: A muddy river reaches a calm lake and the water clears. What happened to the mud? | The water slowed and dropped it, which is deposition | The mud dissolved into nothing | The mud floated up into the clouds as steam | The mud turned into fish food at once and was all eaten up
test: What would happen if Earth had no rain, rivers or ice at all? | Erosion would be far slower, as running water is its strongest agent | Mountains would wear down much faster, with nothing to protect them | Deserts would turn into forests | Rocks would grow back together
test: Grandad: "bloody farm next door ripped out all the trees, now the field washes into the road every storm." Why? | Without trees, soil erodes many times faster | Trees cause storms, so removing them is bad | Roads attract soil like a magnet | Soil grows faster without trees
test: A game says mountains last for ever and never change. What is true? | Mountains are slowly worn down over millions of years | Mountains are built new every year | Mountains melt a little every summer and refreeze in winter | Mountains only change in earthquakes
test: Strange mushroom-shaped rocks stand in a sandy desert. What most likely shaped them? | Wind blasting sand against them | Ocean waves that once crashed there | Tree roots growing all round them | Frost in a hot, steamy rainforest
test: Lily: "the rock got broken by frost and then the stream took the bits — that's all weathering." What should she change? | The stream taking the bits is erosion | The frost part is erosion too | Neither part is weathering nor erosion | Streams only drop rocks, never carry them
test: Why are Scotland's mountains so much lower than the Alps today? | They are very old and have been worn down | They were never more than small hills | People cut their tops off for building stone | They are sinking into the sea
exam: Identify the principal distinction between weathering and erosion. | Weathering disintegrates rock in situ, whereas erosion removes the fragments | Weathering occurs solely in winter, whereas erosion occurs in summer | Weathering is caused by animals, whereas erosion is caused by volcanoes | Weathering creates rock, whereas erosion destroys it
exam: Ascertain the agent of erosion acknowledged to be the most powerful upon the Earth. | Running water | Wind in arid regions | Glacial ice in deserts | Burrowing animals
exam: Select the response that is consistent with the established facts concerning deposition. | Material is deposited when the transporting water or wind loses speed | Material is deposited when the water accelerates | Deposition denotes the freezing of water in rock fissures | Deposition denotes the dissolution of limestone
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the consequence of deforestation upon soil? | Erosion of soil may proceed many times more rapidly than at natural rates | Soil is thereby rendered immune to erosion | Soil formation is thereby accelerated beyond all natural rates observed | No consequence whatsoever is observed
